  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x+2}={2}^{x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2} & ={2}^{x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({2}^{x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+2=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({2}^{x+3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+2=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) \\\\ x+2 & =x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2) & =3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)-2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)) & =3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
